In a browser, a developer runs this script on a page that has no other pending work:

```js
console.log("A");
setTimeout(() => console.log("B"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("C"));
console.log("D");
```

Later, a colleague claims that because the timeout is `0`, `B` should be printed before `C`. Using this script, explain what log order the browser actually produces and walk through the scheduling events that cause it. Address why the `0` delay does not make the timer callback run before the promise callback.

